Author: Redhuan D. Oon <103561963+red1oon@users.noreply.github.com>
Date:   Fri Jul 17 22:45:34 2026 +0800

    feat(time-machine): high-quality Movie Export — Alt+S-quality per storyboard beat (#849)
    
    * feat(time-machine): high-quality movie export — Alt+S-quality still per storyboard beat
    
    Spec: bim-compiler prompts/PHOTOREAL_STILL_RENDER.md §IMPLEMENTATION SPEC
    (2026-07-17). One Alt+S-quality still (16-sample TAA + 24-frame N8AO fold)
    captured per _cineStoryboard beat via a direct jump-cut camera placement
    (reuses the Director's own steady-state distance formula, skips its
    tick-based easing/transit machinery — not needed for a discrete stop-motion
    still). Frames land in a dedicated IDB store (bim_ootb_tm_export, not the
    existing small JSON cache), then stitch into a webm via a proxy canvas +
    MediaRecorder, reusing Cinema Orbit's exact capture pattern.
    
    Additive changes to existing files:
    - effects.js: A.startStillRefine(onComplete) — optional completion callback
      threaded through the AO/SSGI fold, fired on every exit path (including
      early bails), so a batch caller can await genuine completion instead of
      polling console output. No callback passed = zero behavior change.
    - main.js: gate the incidental-touch-cancel wiring (pointerdown/wheel/
      controls-start -> stopStillRefine/softStopStillRefine) behind
      !APP._tmExportActive — an unattended hour-long export must not be torn
      down by a stray mouse move while checking progress.
    - time_machine.js: replaces #tm-share (a low-value link-copy button once
      this exists in the same slot, per user's own framing) with Export Movie /
      Cancel Export.
    
    Two real bugs found and fixed via headless end-to-end testing (not assumed):
    (1) stopStillRefine()'s 500ms interaction-nudge grace window silently
    no-op'd the teardown between beats when a fold finished fast, leaving
    _stillRefineActive stuck true and freezing the next beat's capture — fixed
    by passing force=true, the parameter's own intended escape hatch for a
    deliberate (non-accidental) programmatic stop. (2) Cancelling mid-fold
    resolved the capture promise without ever calling stopStillRefine(),
    leaving the still-refine/staging state stuck applied until an unrelated
    later interaction happened to touch the canvas — fixed by tearing down
    explicitly on the cancel path too.
    
    Verified headless (puppeteer, SwiftShader, warehouse_gardenworld, 3-beat
    storyboard): full export produces a valid webm via the real #tm-export
    button click, zero page errors; separately verified Cancel Export mid-run
    tears down cleanly (_stillRefineActive false, button reverts, no crash on
    a post-cancel interaction). sw.js CACHE_VERSION bumped v778->v779 (all
    four touched files are cache-first precached assets).
    
    Not pushed — standing localhost-only push-pause.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(time-machine): log every tmStartMovieExport bail path, not just the toast
    
    User reported clicking Export Movie and seeing zero related console output —
    tracked it to viewerStatus() being DOM-only (no console.log), so every early
    bail (pipeline unavailable, already running, no storyboard scenes) was a
    genuinely silent no-op. Violates this repo's own Log Mandate ("SKIP and
    silent failures only appear in the log"). Added §TM_EXPORT click on entry
    and §TM_EXPORT_ABORT reason=<...> on each bail, with enough state in the
    reason to diagnose without re-asking the user what they clicked.
    
    sw.js CACHE_VERSION v779->v780 (time_machine.js is precached).
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * fix(history): §WHOLE-LANDED logs page origin, not just db= — bug reports need both
    
    User's ghosting report cost real back-and-forth confirming which deployment
    was even being tested (turned out to be production GH Pages, not any local
    worktree) because this log line only ever showed the db= URL, never the
    page's own origin — a local session can legitimately point db= at
    production OCI data, so db= alone doesn't disambiguate.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
